-- title:
--   The p-adic Eisenstein torsion of J₀(p) vanishes
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $m$ be a natural number. Write $\mathrm{HeckeAlg}$ for the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Z}[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ on indeterminates indexed by the primes, acting on $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$, the degree-zero Picard group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular function field of level $p$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, through the module structure `heckeModuleBar p` (evaluation of the indeterminates at the Hecke operators when these are known to commute, and the structure induced by sending all indeterminates to $0$ otherwise). Let $\mathfrak{P}_p =$ `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p p` be the ideal of $\mathrm{HeckeAlg}$ consisting of those polynomials whose image under `eisensteinEval p` lies in the ideal generated by $p$ in the target, i.e. the preimage of $(p)$ under that evaluation map. The theorem asserts that the $\mathfrak{P}_p^m$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$ — the subgroup of elements annihilated by every element of the $m$-th power $\mathfrak{P}_p^m$ — is the trivial subgroup $\bot$.
--
--   This is the statement that the Eisenstein primes of the Hecke algebra acting on $J_0(p)$ have residue characteristic prime to $p$, so that no Eisenstein maximal ideal of residue characteristic $p$ supports torsion; in Mazur's terms, the Eisenstein primes divide the numerator $n$ of $(p-1)/12$, and $p \nmid n$. It is used in the counting of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points in the primary torsion filtration of $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$, via [`ModularCurve.JZeroNeronPrimaryTorsionFlag.natCard_algHom_succ_eq_mul_natCard_algHom_hopfKer`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZeroNeronPrimaryTorsionFlag.natCard_algHom_succ_eq_mul_natCard_algHom_hopfKer).
